<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Chaeronea (338 BCE)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/338-bce">338 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Chaeronea was fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> in August 338 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and an alliance led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>. The Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> and cavalry, the latter commanded by the young Alexander, destroyed the allied army and the Theban Sacred Band. The victory gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> control of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, organised soon afterwards in the League of Corinth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce.jpg" alt="Battle of Chaeronea (338 BCE)" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kirill Lokshin; derivative work by Dipa_1965 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Chaeronea,_338_BC_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Athens, urged on by Demosthenes, joined Thebes against Philip II of Macedon and was defeated at the <b>Battle of Chaeronea</b> in 338 BCE; Philip treated the city leniently, and it joined his League of Corinth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of <b>Chaeronea</b> in 338 BCE Philip II of Macedon defeated Thebes and Athens, and the Sacred Band was destroyed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A Fourth Sacred War against Amphissa in 339 BCE brought Philip south again and led to the <b>Battle of Chaeronea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Victory at <b>Chaeronea</b> in 338 BCE and the League of Corinth that followed made Philip master of Greece, positioning Macedon for the unprecedented expansion realised by his son, Alexander the Great (r. 336–323 BCE), whose conquests carried Macedonian authority across Persia and into Egypt and India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Third Sacred War against Phocis (356-346 BCE) brought Philip II of Macedon onto the council, and the Fourth (339 BCE) led to his victory at <b>Chaeronea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating Athens and Thebes at <b>Chaeronea</b> in 338 BCE, Philip II called the Greek states to Corinth in 338-337 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-corinth">League of Corinth</a></li></ul></section>
